#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4791998a-2e12-50fa-8127-8fe3482a030d" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Maclaurin Series of a Function in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b107bb37-6227-51b1-a0f5-efa18fa75e8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n+2}}{(n+1)!}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400185} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb12437-5f1e-55c9-b309-294b4178c0e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2(n+1)}}{(2n+1)!}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400186} {:answer/id #uuid "83f1386c-36d2-50ae-adc9-51ebace27333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400187} {:answer/id #uuid "3a0fa805-7feb-5062-90a9-7b06c76dca32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{2n}}{(2n+1)!}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400188} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b5ebd4-56b7-5c92-bfe5-69700bbad247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2(n+1)}}{(2n)!}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbb12437-5f1e-55c9-b309-294b4178c0e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2(n+1)}}{(2n+1)!}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400186} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d66455f-0c62-5cd9-9133-6e646aa1100a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66708e85-e31b-5700-a1e3-46ef23b1df08" :question/math-academy-id "q-125335" :question/problem "Express the Maclaurin series expansion for $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ given by\n\n$$\nx\\operatorname{sin}⁡x={x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{3!}+\\frac{{x}^{6}}{5!}-\\frac{{x}^{8}}{7!}+⋯\n$$\n\nusing sigma notation." :question/worked-solution "The first four terms of the series are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{3!} \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{6}}{5!} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =-\\frac{{x}^{8}}{7!}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can express these terms using a common format, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{(-1)^{0}{x}^{2\\cdot (0+1)}}{(2\\cdot 0+1)!} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{(-1)^{1}{x}^{2\\cdot (1+1)}}{(2\\cdot 1+1)!} \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}{x}^{2\\cdot (2+1)}}{(2\\cdot 2+1)!} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{(-1)^{3}{x}^{2\\cdot (3+1)}}{(2\\cdot 3+1)!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe deduce that the general formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2(n+1)}}{(2n+1)!}\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2(n+1)}}{(2n+1)!}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed7286fe-b6e2-5544-8bc6-86afc6cdb813" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Matrix by Its Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3912714-af31-5641-8bff-9b46c1aa3234" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400192} {:answer/id #uuid "4ceb3c00-a293-5837-a92d-52eeb50f0ae2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400193} {:answer/id #uuid "e1eca2de-1a70-5b67-a36e-991a07dbc939" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400194} {:answer/id #uuid "9f38d46b-1ede-55d8-a14d-a43663a75ca9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400195} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab42314-64ed-5e2f-b8e6-3a69d859b6fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3912714-af31-5641-8bff-9b46c1aa3234" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400192} :answer-field/id #uuid "441fdc82-6754-52e3-ae25-4987eecf3c9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b30b956-8f97-5e5a-bc88-28b6d6148226" :question/math-academy-id "q-125549" :question/problem "Which of the following is a $2\\times 2$ matrix?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $P$ has $2$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 2.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- The matrix $Q$ has $4$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $4\\times 2.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $R$ has $2$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 2.$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, only $P$ and $R$ are $2\\times 2$ matrices."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400197 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecd07ea0-767d-55f0-a61c-da49dd320f89" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Entry of a Square Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a92aa67e-ecfa-5bfe-ab81-ddf48f5beaa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such entry" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400199} {:answer/id #uuid "eef484db-8fa8-5f4b-a540-416267185b92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400200} {:answer/id #uuid "7149fefe-50ce-50a1-bf7e-38d9e1beb3a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400201} {:answer/id #uuid "6fdc8317-2035-5b4f-8a63-f17ca589311b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400202} {:answer/id #uuid "b50586f8-7d5f-5f88-9732-94c927142de2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a92aa67e-ecfa-5bfe-ab81-ddf48f5beaa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such entry" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400199} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d60e189-2c30-5a72-be7e-bf7345acd2ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400198}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf0cde7f-5b94-5af7-b3da-c7304b62c190" :question/math-academy-id "q-125564" :question/problem "Find the entry ${b}_{32}$ of the matrix $B$, where \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The entry ${b}_{32}$ should lie at the intersection of the $3$rd row and $2$nd column. \nSince $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, it does not have the $3$rd row. \n\nTherefore, $B$ does not have the entry ${b}_{32}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400204 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cc70bda-40cf-5860-9a47-25f290c71041" :knowledge-point/title "Describing an Entry of a Matrix in Index Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83bdeed3-df37-5030-9210-4fd26c862b5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{31}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400206} {:answer/id #uuid "5071b299-4f24-5216-a03b-d3bb38f10c37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{41}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400207} {:answer/id #uuid "6c05e69c-1e2c-58a5-8d1d-ec8b55b911ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400208} {:answer/id #uuid "7d163472-1ec4-5e60-97dc-1403c415123e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{42}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400209} {:answer/id #uuid "214dd131-7e3a-56a7-b7fd-be2f662d3fd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{43}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d163472-1ec4-5e60-97dc-1403c415123e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{42}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400209} :answer-field/id #uuid "93e2aeb3-64f5-5d7d-b868-6bf0abe7eba8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a14b13fa-47dd-5e1a-8086-20e6009f70ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-125611" :question/problem "Consider the highlighted entry of the matrix $Y$ given below. Describe it in terms of ${y}_{ij}.$\n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The entry lies at the intersection of the $4$th row and $2$nd column. Hence, we have ${y}_{42}=7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400211 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "09fa0899-a3d2-584f-8cdd-a471f8cfd16b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Special Square Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "553e0164-69a4-571b-83c5-465b3b54c747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400213} {:answer/id #uuid "340d28a9-ac24-51ee-a63d-8b51e38606b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400214} {:answer/id #uuid "78a35f41-30f5-5c39-b8bd-a9e3ffc633f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400215} {:answer/id #uuid "21a84219-2aac-5deb-8cdc-11cd02b9b263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400216} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e6c06f-a2ea-57cc-a6b1-d8e81a150186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "340d28a9-ac24-51ee-a63d-8b51e38606b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400214} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7e2e824-6270-5a22-9f66-f3228d3b14f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8674af78-bd5e-58b6-917b-e86f01702cc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-126222" :question/problem "Which of the following are diagonal matrices?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0\\text{ }\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- $P$ is *not* a diagonal matrix since it's not a square matrix.\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a diagonal matrix since it's not a square matrix.\n\n- $R$ is *not* a diagonal matrix. It's a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. But some of the entries that do not lie on the main diagonal are not zeros (for instance, ${r}_{31}=7\\ne 0$).\n\nTherefore, none of the given are diagonal matrices."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ca13445-0218-5950-967e-a68bcfec32c7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Square Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a1baaae-52d0-516b-a600-797b0388a127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400220} {:answer/id #uuid "58de69df-cd32-5f8f-af98-12f470c70f21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400221} {:answer/id #uuid "a552f5cd-8b45-5e90-b478-e07b0a27d36d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400222} {:answer/id #uuid "89ac50ef-c4ba-518c-8394-d2674f51bbdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400223} {:answer/id #uuid "78dd51ac-64a5-503e-bd20-319def136b11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89ac50ef-c4ba-518c-8394-d2674f51bbdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400223} :answer-field/id #uuid "9197f330-4826-5c7c-93a0-be4b4589ea86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1fe65ee-e4c1-507a-9313-0ea6b012c1aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-126278" :question/problem "Which of the following are square matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- $A$ is a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. It has $1$ row and $1$ column, so it is a $1\\times 1$ matrix.\n\n- $B$ is a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. It has $3$ rows and $3$ columns, so it is a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n- $C$ is *not* a square matrix since the numbers of rows and columns are not the same. It has $1$ row and $2$ columns, so it's a $1\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $B$ are square matrices."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b6ddadb-471a-5195-a0f5-fd44bdc8f780" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Other Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf4c3781-1b67-53e0-91c6-9593e072014d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400227} {:answer/id #uuid "bac3ee2b-f690-591f-b3e6-5fdd75c9a5dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400228} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc6769f-f67a-553d-839b-096df4607353" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400229} {:answer/id #uuid "fc536d94-62f6-5d74-84b2-f2009b1d5026" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400230} {:answer/id #uuid "8dde38bd-46a0-5152-8b2b-5e3f164418ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8dde38bd-46a0-5152-8b2b-5e3f164418ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400231} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5d68012-89d6-5afa-af0d-db48f598febd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9fca4a1-9999-5571-b7b3-a6f45eb203a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-126287" :question/problem "Which of the following are column vectors?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- $A$ is *not* a column vector. It has only one row (and $2$ columns), so it's a row vector.\n\n- $B$ is a column vector since it has only one column.\n\n- $C$ is *not* a column vector, since it has more than $1$ column (it has $2$ columns).\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is a column vector."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f71a8f16-93d4-5e5a-9b8b-e29efc682430" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reflection of a Cosine Curve Over the Horizontal Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4e173d4-f5b8-5ccc-9008-d91b45244f8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1.5cos⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400234} {:answer/id #uuid "4c24a715-ead1-58d4-8292-0189666d823a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=cos⁡(x)-1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400235} {:answer/id #uuid "c1cd17dd-96e3-5b25-bf97-b5e326ff0ab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=cos⁡(x)+1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400236} {:answer/id #uuid "774b4b4b-55ca-5041-80c5-d41b0058a5c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-cos⁡(x-1.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400237} {:answer/id #uuid "c37c2d1e-7ba4-57eb-9816-faa0ae5b47ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1.5cos⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c37c2d1e-7ba4-57eb-9816-faa0ae5b47ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1.5cos⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400238} :answer-field/id #uuid "56e9c54d-acd0-56fe-8076-95dec659fb84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5183ff04-e16d-572d-aca9-41a49c02cc01" :question/math-academy-id "q-12675" :question/problem "![](images/14/146cd919d8b1cdc034dc8aa1d8d0867bee7475b7ed72837c935de3cd11b38d59.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the graph shown above?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/85/857a4b0109ddc20535b65ffe16191494ab9fdef01b6def354fcd68b862b98679.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-cos⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $1.5$ to give $y=-1.5cos⁡x$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-1.5cos⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "beb5fa66-9dc2-50aa-beaa-310da7fc4c6b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Interval of Convergence of a Given Power Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b515481-27aa-50cd-a614-2b3b3c8e9916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-3,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400241} {:answer/id #uuid "70cb914f-6e67-52a8-8099-833f6e52d19a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-3,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400242} {:answer/id #uuid "246bb1dc-7057-5890-bb32-5ae992573b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400243} {:answer/id #uuid "a615fbac-40a1-5a96-905b-a1b1e6c1fe27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400244} {:answer/id #uuid "92822cb5-bcbd-5e16-981e-b28bb3550efb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "246bb1dc-7057-5890-bb32-5ae992573b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400243} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e1c6021-571a-58eb-ae1a-ae2e4f0fcf7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e567d72e-c85f-58b7-9ff5-c8e3c41c5382" :question/math-academy-id "q-126774" :question/problem "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}}=\\frac{3x}{{1}^{3/2}}-\\frac{{3}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{2}^{3/2}}+\\frac{{3}^{3}{x}^{3}}{{3}^{3/2}}-\\frac{{3}^{4}{x}^{4}}{{4}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+2}{3}^{n+1}{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{3/2}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{3/2}}{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{-3x{n}^{3/2}}{(n+1)^{3/2}}| \\\\  & =3|-x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n}{n+1})}^{3/2} \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for $|x|<\\frac{1}{3}.$ So, the radius of convergence is $\\frac{1}{3},$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3}).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=\\frac{1}{3},$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{(\\frac{3}{3})}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{4}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.   So the endpoint $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(-1)^{n}{(\\frac{3}{3})}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{2n+1}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-(1+\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{3/2}}+⋯)\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the $p$-series test.   So the endpoint $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "606b9a5e-55ed-5e60-92f5-b47ee5f67ecd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400248} {:answer/id #uuid "d6dd58b2-db2a-5f8e-8dd4-53e78bdbc1b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,1⟩+u⟨-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400249} {:answer/id #uuid "857fe3f7-9574-5a04-bb5d-26945b5b3ac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400250} {:answer/id #uuid "d79b2f0b-232c-51be-8226-2f1f3340e84c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400251} {:answer/id #uuid "34207664-1cc3-511a-9275-7f215d28db11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,1⟩+u⟨0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "606b9a5e-55ed-5e60-92f5-b47ee5f67ecd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400248} :answer-field/id #uuid "15cc7274-0fbf-5ba4-9530-45869135aca7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b063a87b-7453-5eed-82c3-d4c7e3cbe51f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126838" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ at the point where $t=\\pi ,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=\\pi ,$ the vector ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(\\pi )+u{r}^{′}(\\pi ),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point where $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨0,1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a35b6d-2cae-582e-8493-f87cca09de5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1⟩+u⟨2,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400255} {:answer/id #uuid "89946061-3179-5a62-92a4-c8e5a2b9e7b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨2,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400256} {:answer/id #uuid "4d287fc9-fb22-5b2f-ba9a-16affbe14ca8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1⟩+u⟨0,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400257} {:answer/id #uuid "09e9c576-9966-5982-b9d6-7c94b62a04c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1⟩+u⟨2,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400258} {:answer/id #uuid "c517880b-005f-5788-8339-983446cb7ed1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨0,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d287fc9-fb22-5b2f-ba9a-16affbe14ca8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1⟩+u⟨0,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400257} :answer-field/id #uuid "19d09d6d-fdd3-564f-8577-1f47d907f393" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1276b48a-33df-5447-9033-1c8e5ee23a8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126841" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+1),1-5t⟩$ at the point where $t=0,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=0,$ the vector ${r}^{′}(0)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(0)+u{r}^{′}(0),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡({0}^{2}+1),1-5(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+1)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-5t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+1},-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0) & =⟨\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+1},-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨0,1⟩+u⟨0,-5⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3146eb6-b4ef-554d-83c1-351a3fb15651" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Terms of the Taylor Series of a Function About a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "549a5daf-5666-553f-82f5-27205446194a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{1}{8}(x-2)^{4}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400262} {:answer/id #uuid "c59d759c-090a-569e-a841-2f3f881a6cf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)-\\frac{1}{4}(x-2)^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400263} {:answer/id #uuid "81b3de1b-3b6d-5810-a6e2-9923ca999f44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)-\\frac{1}{8}(x-2)^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400264} {:answer/id #uuid "12b9fcee-9cc1-5cd7-9dcf-0488b3381528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-(x-2)+\\frac{1}{4}(x-2)^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400265} {:answer/id #uuid "49ec3922-a5e3-5d3a-8e40-816bba0da596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+(x-2)-\\frac{1}{3}(x-2)^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81b3de1b-3b6d-5810-a6e2-9923ca999f44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)-\\frac{1}{8}(x-2)^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400264} :answer-field/id #uuid "9533d8c7-2fb9-5174-8f0a-9eef9e450415" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b7eb3fc-d271-5f71-a892-30b0a27230ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-126844" :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)$ about the point $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)$ and $a=2.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ f(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot 2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+(\\frac{1}{2})(x-2)+\\frac{1}{2!}(-\\frac{1}{4})(x-2)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)-\\frac{1}{8}(x-2)^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd21ee24-c0d9-5621-afc6-6d27d5667671" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡3=0.141\\,37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400269} {:answer/id #uuid "b855efbc-015a-516c-8623-2f49d81ccdfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡3=0.141\\,24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400270} {:answer/id #uuid "4050c6eb-6955-5ab8-9e30-4b45a04a133c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡3=0.141\\,05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400271} {:answer/id #uuid "e685dbbf-0847-56b5-93b8-5e55c10ab626" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡3=0.141\\,12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400272} {:answer/id #uuid "1901a0c4-6458-5e91-a94f-d4a3599e24dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡3=0.141\\,19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e685dbbf-0847-56b5-93b8-5e55c10ab626" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡3=0.141\\,12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400272} :answer-field/id #uuid "5265398a-cfae-5735-aad4-474a4cb4c335" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "001d0357-fa46-5892-877e-9b6cc4712426" :question/math-academy-id "q-126874" :question/problem "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ about $x=\\pi $ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡3.$ Give your answer to $5$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\pi .$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi =0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =-1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi =0 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =(0)+(-1)(x-\\pi )+\\frac{(0)}{2!}(x-\\pi )^{2}+\\frac{(1)}{3!}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =-(x-\\pi )+\\frac{1}{6}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=3$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{sin}⁡3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3 & ≈-(3-\\pi )+\\frac{1}{6}(3-\\pi )^{3} \\\\  & =0.141\\,12,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto five decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "813ea027-299b-510a-86a9-2e76ce0edeec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.739" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400276} {:answer/id #uuid "81e7917c-2e81-580f-949d-985ad24febfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.735" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400277} {:answer/id #uuid "aee0f806-2311-54bd-9a9b-bb6e04c3b17d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.744" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400278} {:answer/id #uuid "8a4972b2-7545-5695-be8e-1e853fe55f32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.746" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400279} {:answer/id #uuid "1c2db0c1-d929-521e-913f-2d2d27ccf71e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.732" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81e7917c-2e81-580f-949d-985ad24febfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.735" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400277} :answer-field/id #uuid "4855ece8-350c-52ea-af76-5afe755310a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fcccafb-96d2-5248-a5bc-581af0a17196" :question/math-academy-id "q-126877" :question/problem "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}).$ Give your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =1+(2)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{(4)}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) & ≈1+2(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2{(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =1+2(-\\frac{\\pi }{20})+2{(-\\frac{\\pi }{20})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{200} \\\\  & =0.735\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c338d1eb-7e13-5089-8dd3-d5ca6eefd4ba" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points on a Line That Are a Fixed Distance From Another Point on the Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a4dba27-04b2-5c7c-a0f4-65dfb83678d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,-1,-7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400283} {:answer/id #uuid "c9fa9a5b-f01b-53ec-95e1-597f44339eae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(-1,1,7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400284} {:answer/id #uuid "b58591df-e0d8-522a-a4de-ebba4b8f0e74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(-1,-1,7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,-5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400285} {:answer/id #uuid "358f67ac-2cdf-5196-b078-18b1f8cb7e02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,1,7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,-5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400286} {:answer/id #uuid "ceeeb864-562e-5444-a903-bc04d0d2fb6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(-1,1,-7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9fa9a5b-f01b-53ec-95e1-597f44339eae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(-1,1,7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400284} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8304411-0971-51d5-bb97-446c83b8db6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "59a52ddc-339d-53d5-a5c4-8b8787ceb738" :question/math-academy-id "q-126995" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(2,3,6)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-3,-2,1⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP=\\sqrt{14}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨2,3,6⟩+t⟨-3,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨2,3,6⟩+t⟨-3,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨2,3,6⟩+t⟨-3,-2,1⟩)-⟨2,3,6⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨-3,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3t,\\,-2t,\\,t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=\\sqrt{14},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ |⟨-3t,\\,-2t,\\,t⟩| & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{(-3t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2}+{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\pm t\\sqrt{14} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ t & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 1$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨2,3,6⟩+⟨-3,-2,1⟩=⟨-1,1,7⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨2,3,6⟩-⟨-3,-2,1⟩=⟨5,5,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(-1,1,7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,5,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f232aed9-1584-5e57-a881-226a6876384a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Straight Line Given Parametrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1463e0e7-f3c4-5b6d-8d96-6a85602fe7c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400290} {:answer/id #uuid "eb2a0c4a-1049-5b74-b656-3842fe7f5d7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400291} {:answer/id #uuid "1283f67a-2c6a-5f62-80ed-b95b34eead73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400292} {:answer/id #uuid "dc2f2b02-c259-5875-921e-9a147504b486" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400293} {:answer/id #uuid "ad87